\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The circle in the same terms}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{For cycle decimation, \ref{fdip-0057} and \ref{fdip-00AY}
give the exact coordinate #{\eta\mapsto q\eta}, with shape
#{\cosh\eta}. Hence #{\eta^2\mapsto q^2\eta^2} exactly.
The shape #{\eta=0} is fixed. It is not a normalized Gaussian
probability on the full massless field space, and raw decimation
does not automatically preserve a mean-zero constraint; the
constrained probabilities are those of \ref{fdip-003V}.}

\p{The shape #{\cosh(m/N)} has #{\eta=m/N}, so it follows the
[line of constant physics](fdip-01D3). The representatives in
\ref{fdip-00FY} and \ref{fdip-00FZ} are exact for decimation.
The convergence of block means in \ref{fdip-00G1} is an
asymptotic statement, and does not assert that those representatives
are exact fixed points of block averaging.}

\p{There is a separate linear scaling comparison for the grid
symbol. Under #{\alpha(\theta)\mapsto q^2\alpha(\theta /q)},
a homogeneous Taylor term of degree #{2j} is multiplied by
#{q^{2-2j}}. Thus the quadratic term has multiplier #{1},
the constant mass term has multiplier #{q^2}, and the terms
of degree #{4} or higher have multipliers smaller than #{1}
when #{q>1}. Equivalently, in
#{N^2\alpha(2\pi\kappa /N)} the degree-#{2j} term carries
#{N^{2-2j}}. This is a scaling statement about symbols, not
closure of the finite-range family under box averaging. In the
bare cycle coordinate #{2(\cosh\eta -1)}, #{q^2} is only the
linearized multiplier at zero; it is exact for #{\eta^2}.}

\p{The use of contracting linear couplings in this comparison
agrees with \citet{Section 5.1, equation (5.3), p.112}{wilson1974renormalization}.
The exact finite identities and the grid convergence statements
here are the calculations above.}
